<section class="section reveal" id="why-oop">
  <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
  <h2>Why Object-Oriented Programming?</h2>

  <p>As programs grow larger, organizing code becomes critical. Imagine building an image classification system. You need to manage image loading, preprocessing, model inference, postprocessing, and results storage. Without organization, these responsibilities scatter throughout your codebase, making it hard to test, reuse, and maintain.</p>

  <p>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) provides a framework for organizing code by bundling related data (state) and operations (behavior) together. Every modern AI framework uses OOP:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>PyTorch:</strong> Models inherit from <code>nn.Module</code>, datasets inherit from <code>Dataset</code></li>
    <li><strong>TensorFlow/Keras:</strong> Layers inherit from <code>Layer</code>, models inherit from <code>Model</code></li>
    <li><strong>scikit-learn:</strong> All estimators follow the <code>fit()</code>-<code>predict()</code> interface via classes</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>A class is like a blueprint for a house. The blueprint specifies the structure, but it is not the house itself. When you instantiate the blueprint (build the house), you create an object — a concrete house with specific rooms, furniture, and decorations. Multiple houses can be built from the same blueprint, each unique but all following the same design.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="infographic reveal">
