<section class="section reveal" id="inheritance">
  <span class="section-label">Advanced Concepts</span>
  <h2>Inheritance and Polymorphism</h2>

  <p>Inheritance lets you create specialized classes from a base class. This is how PyTorch models work: they inherit from <code>nn.Module</code>. Polymorphism means different classes can respond to the same method call in their own way.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Base class</span>
<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">BaseModel</span>:
    <span class="st">"""Abstract base class for ML models."""</span>
    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(<span class="va">self</span>, <span class="va">name</span>):
        <span class="va">self</span>.<span class="va">name</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">name</span>

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">train</span>(<span class="va">self</span>):
        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">NotImplementedError</span>()

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">predict</span>(<span class="va">self</span>, <span class="va">x</span>):
        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">NotImplementedError</span>()

<span class="cm"># Specialized subclasses</span>
<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">RandomForest</span>(<span class="fn">BaseModel</span>):
    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">train</span>(<span class="va">self</span>):
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Training </span>{<span class="va">self</span>.<span class="va">name</span>}<span class="st"> trees..."</span>)

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">predict</span>(<span class="va">self</span>, <span class="va">x</span>):
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="st">"class_0"</span>

<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">NeuralNet</span>(<span class="fn">BaseModel</span>):
    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">train</span>(<span class="va">self</span>):
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Training </span>{<span class="va">self</span>.<span class="va">name</span>}<span class="st"> backprop..."</span>)

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">predict</span>(<span class="va">self</span>, <span class="va">x</span>):
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="st">"class_1"</span>

<span class="cm"># Polymorphism in action</span>
<span class="va">models</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="fn">RandomForest</span>(<span class="st">"RF"</span>), <span class="fn">NeuralNet</span>(<span class="st">"MLP"</span>)]
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">model</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">models</span>:
    <span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">train</span>()  <span class="cm"># Each calls its own implementation</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> <code>RandomForest</code> and <code>NeuralNet</code> inherit from <code>BaseModel</code>. Each overrides <code>train()</code> and <code>predict()</code> with their own implementations. Polymorphism means we can call <code>train()</code> on any model without knowing its type. The right implementation is called automatically.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Using super() to Access Parent Methods</h3>
  <p>Sometimes you want to extend a parent's method without replacing it. Use <code>super()</code>:</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">RegularizedModel</span>(<span class="fn">BaseModel</span>):
    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(<span class="va">self</span>, <span class="va">name</span>, <span class="va">l2_penalty</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.01</span>):
        <span class="fn">super</span>().__span class="fn">__init__</span>(<span class="va">name</span>)
        <span class="va">self</span>.<span class="va">l2_penalty</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">l2_penalty</span>

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">train</span>(<span class="va">self</span>):
        <span class="fn">super</span>().<span class="fn">train</span>()  <span class="cm"># Could call parent if implemented</span>
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Applying L2 penalty: </span>{<span class="va">self</span>.<span class="va">l2_penalty</span>}<span class="st">"</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> <code>super().__init__(name)</code> calls the parent's constructor, setting the name attribute. Then we add our own attributes. This is standard practice in ML frameworks like PyTorch.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In PyTorch:</strong> Every model inherits from <code>nn.Module</code>. You must call <code>super().__init__()</code> in your model's <code>__init__</code>. Understanding this pattern is essential to using modern ML frameworks effectively.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Inheritance creates specialized classes from a base class</li>
      <li>Polymorphism allows different classes to respond to the same method differently</li>
      <li>Use <code>super()</code> to call parent class methods and constructors</li>
      <li>Override methods to customize behavior while maintaining the interface</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="section-divider">

<section class="section reveal" id="special-methods">
  <span class="section-label">Best Practices</span>
  <h2>Special Methods and Pythonic Classes</h2>

  <p>Special methods (dunder methods) let your classes work with Python's built-in syntax. Implement <code>__len__</code> to use <code>len()</code>, <code>__str__</code> for <code>print()</code>, and <code>__getitem__</code> for indexing:</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">Dataset</span>:
    <span class="st">"""Dataset with standard Python behavior."""</span>
    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(<span class="va">self</span>, <span class="va">data</span>: <span class="fn">list</span>, <span class="va">labels</span>: <span class="fn">list</span>):
        <span class="va">self</span>.<span class="va">data</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">data</span>
        <span class="va">self</span>.<span class="va">labels</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">labels</span>

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__len__</span>(<span class="va">self</span>) <span class="op">-></span> <span class="fn">int</span>:
        <span class="st">"""Return dataset size."""</span>
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">self</span>.<span class="va">data</span>)

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__getitem__</span>(<span class="va">self</span>, <span class="va">idx</span>: <span class="fn">int</span>):
        <span class="st">"""Support indexing with dataset[i]."""</span>
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="va">self</span>.<span class="va">data</span>[<span class="va">idx</span>], <span class="va">self</span>.<span class="va">labels</span>[<span class="va">idx</span>]

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__str__</span>(<span class="va">self</span>) <span class="op">-></span> <span class="fn">str</span>:
        <span class="st">"""User-friendly representation."""</span>
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="st">f"Dataset(</span>{<span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">self</span>)}<span class="st"> samples)"</span>

<span class="cm"># Use like built-in types</span>
<span class="va">ds</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">Dataset</span>([<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>], [<span class="st">'a'</span>, <span class="st">'b'</span>, <span class="st">'c'</span>])
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">ds</span>))        <span class="cm"># 3</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">ds</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>])         <span class="cm"># (1, 'a')</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">ds</span>)           <span class="cm"># Dataset(3 samples)</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> Special methods make custom classes feel natural. We can use <code>len()</code>, indexing, and print() directly on our objects. PyTorch datasets must implement <code>__len__</code> and <code>__getitem__</code> for the data loader to work.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Encapsulation and Property Decorators</h3>
  <p>Hide internal details with the <code>@property</code> decorator. This lets you control how attributes are accessed and modified:</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">Model</span>:
    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(<span class="va">self</span>):
        <span class="va">self</span>.<span class="op">_</span><span class="va">accuracy</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">0.0</span>  <span class="cm"># Private by convention (_)</span>

    <span class="op">@</span><span class="fn">property</span>
    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">accuracy</span>(<span class="va">self</span>):
        <span class="st">"""Get accuracy."""</span>
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="va">self</span>.<span class="op">_</span><span class="va">accuracy</span>

    <span class="op">@</span><span class="va">accuracy</span>.<span class="fn">setter</span>
    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">accuracy</span>(<span class="va">self</span>, <span class="va">value</span>):
        <span class="st">"""Set accuracy (with validation)."""</span>
        <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="kw">not</span> (<span class="nm">0</span> <span class="op"><=</span> <span class="va">value</span> <span class="op"><=</span> <span class="nm">1</span>):
            <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">ValueError</span>(<span class="st">"Must be in [0, 1]"</span>)
        <span class="va">self</span>.<span class="op">_</span><span class="va">accuracy</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">value</span>

<span class="va">m</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">Model</span>()
<span class="va">m</span>.<span class="va">accuracy</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">0.92</span>  <span class="cm"># Validates automatically</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">m</span>.<span class="va">accuracy</span>)  <span class="cm"># 0.92</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> <code>@property</code> makes <code>accuracy</code> look like a simple attribute to users, but internally runs custom code. The setter validates that accuracy is in [0, 1]. This is encapsulation: hide details, control access, prevent invalid states.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Special methods (__len__, __getitem__, __str__) enable Pythonic syntax</li>
      <li>Implement <code>__len__</code> and <code>__getitem__</code> for datasets to work with PyTorch</li>
      <li>Use @property decorators to control attribute access and add validation</li>
      <li>Encapsulation (hiding details) makes classes more robust and easier to maintain</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

